# sed 4.10.bcr.1: the BCR overlay was written against an earlier rules_cc_autoconf
# whose gnulib ports published some results differently. The ports now follow
# gnulib itself, which changes how the source conditions have to be spelled:
#
#   - A HAVE_X operand (bare or as `ac_cv_subst_HAVE_X`) may resolve to the
#     *_H_DEFAULTS substitution, which is always 1, instead of the value the
#     function's module lowers. Where the port's probe (`ac_cv_func_x`,
#     `ac_cv_have_decl_x`, ...) is visible it is used instead: it is unique
#     and always carries a value, mirroring gnulib's own
#     `test $ac_cv_func_x = no` tests.
#   - HAVE_MBRTOWC, HAVE_NL_LANGINFO and HAVE_READLINK come from
#     AC_CHECK_FUNCS_ONCE, i.e. cache variables ac_cv_func_<name>, exactly as
#     modules/{mbrtowc,nl_langinfo,readlink} test `$HAVE_<NAME> = 0`.
#   - REPLACE_STRERROR_0 is a shell variable (plus an AC_DEFINE when strerror(0)
#     is broken) in m4/strerror.m4; it was never AC_SUBSTed. The port keeps the
#     probe result as `_gl_cv_func_strerror_0_works`, so
#       test -n "$ERRNO_H" || test $REPLACE_STRERROR_0 = 1
#     becomes `GL_GENERATE_ERRNO_H==true || !_gl_cv_func_strerror_0_works`.
#     (GL_GENERATE_ERRNO_H holds the words `true`/`false`, both truthy, so it
#     has to be compared.)
#
# Remove once the overlay on the Bazel Central Registry is updated against a
# rules_cc_autoconf release that contains these ports.
